---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../../components/UiLabel.astro";
import HelpCard from "../../components/HelpCard.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 5: reading estimates — the confidence tiers, the
// "not enough history" state, the irregular-cycles framing, and the late
// resolver. Every explanation of the prediction engine is the in-app help
// card rendered from the export (one home per explanation); tier labels
// and buttons come from the ARB through <UiLabel>; every factual claim
// carries a `<!-- cite: ... -->` ledger entry checked by
// `site/scripts/check-claims.mjs`.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Reading estimates"
  description="What lunarlog's estimate confidence labels mean, why a new profile says there is not enough history yet, and what the irregular-cycles framing changes."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Reading estimates</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      lunarlog's estimate is the average of recent cycle lengths counted
      forward from the last logged period start — an honest guess from
      your own history, and nothing more. It comes with labels that say
      how much to trust it. Estimates only — never medical advice.
    </p>
    <section>
      <h2>What the estimate is</h2>
      <HelpCard id="what-estimate-means" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why a new profile has no estimate</h2>
      <p>
        The app waits for three completed cycles before it estimates
        anything. Until then it says so plainly instead of guessing —
        <UiLabel key="overviewWhyThreeCompletedCycles" /> is a link, not
        an error, and the calendar caption reads
        <UiLabel key="futureExplainerNoEstimate" />.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (whyNoEstimateYet card); lib/domain/prediction/prediction.dart (statusLabel "not enough history yet"); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (futureExplainerNoEstimate, overviewWhyThreeCompletedCycles) -->
      </p>
      <HelpCard id="why-no-estimate-yet" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The confidence labels</h2>
      <p>
        Every estimate carries one of four labels, and the app summarizes
        each in place. The labels in the app's own words:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceHigh" /> —
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceSummaryHigh" />
        </li>
        <li>
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceLearning" /> —
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceSummaryLearning" />
        </li>
        <li>
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceIrregular" /> —
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceSummaryIrregular" />
        </li>
        <li>
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceProvisional" /> —
          <UiLabel key="cycleConfidenceSummaryProvisional" />
        </li>
      </ul>
      <HelpCard id="confidence-levels" />
      <p>
        The label matters more than the date: whenever the estimate is not
        High confidence, the app shows a range of likely days rather than
        one exact date, and the wider the history spreads, the wider the
        range.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (confidenceLevels, whatEstimateMeans cards) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Irregular cycles</h2>
      <p>
        For cycles that simply vary — a teenager's first years, or
        perimenopause — profiles can carry the
        <UiLabel key="profileIrregularFramingLabel" /> framing:
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        <UiLabel key="profileIrregularFramingHint" />
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a framing switch, not a separate engine: the same honest
        maths, presented as ranges instead of dates, with no late banner
        and no late nudges. Teen care mode turns it on by default until
        cycles steady, and it can be changed any time from
        <UiLabel key="editProfileAction" />.
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (profileIrregularFramingLabel/Hint); lib/domain/care_modes.dart (teen composes irregular framing on); lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (edit profile) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When the estimate passes</h2>
      <p>
        A few days past the estimated date, the app asks what to do
        rather than nagging. It offers three options, in the app's words:
        <UiLabel key="lateResolverLogIt" />,
        <UiLabel key="lateResolverSkipCycle" />, or
        <UiLabel key="lateResolverRemindMe" />. Logging the period closes
        the cycle and everything recomputes; skipping leaves the cycle in
        history but out of the average.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (periodLate, skipCycleOmit cards); lib/ui/overview/late_resolver.dart (three options) -->
      </p>
      <HelpCard id="period-late" />
      <p>
        And the "late" in that card carries the important caveat: the
        estimate is an average, and averages miss. A late estimate is
        arithmetic, not a diagnosis — the card routes worry to a
        clinician, and so does this guide.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (periodLate card; no individualised medical advice rule) -->
      </p>
      <HelpCard id="skip-cycle-omit" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Turning estimates off</h2>
      <p>
        Estimates are a feature, not an obligation. A profile can turn
        them off — <UiLabel key="settingsPredictionsTitle" /> in
        <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> — and keep tracking with no dates
        predicted at all. When cycles vary widely, the app itself may
        suggest exactly that.
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (settingsPredictionsTitle, overviewIrregularSuggestionBody); lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart (estimates toggle) -->
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
